3️⃣ First Missing Positive
📌 Why interviewers ask this

Asked in Google, Amazon, Microsoft

Tests array manipulation + in-place hashing

Simple statement, very tricky logic

Filters strong candidates instantly

🧠 Problem Statement

Given an unsorted integer array, find the smallest missing positive integer.

Constraints:

Time: O(n)

Space: O(1) (in-place)

✨ Example
Example 1
Input: [1, 2, 0]
Output: 3

Example 2
Input: [3, 4, -1, 1]
Output: 2

Example 3
Input: [7, 8, 9, 11, 12]
Output: 1

💡 Key Insight (VERY IMPORTANT)

The answer must lie between 1 and n+1
(n = array length)

So:
👉 We place each number x at index x-1

🧩 Algorithm (In-Place Index Mapping)

Traverse array

While:

nums[i] is in range 1..n

AND nums[i] not already in correct position
👉 Swap it with nums[nums[i] - 1]

After placement:

Traverse array again

First index i where nums[i] != i+1 → answer = i+1

If all correct → return n+1

✅ Java Code
public class FirstMissingPositive {

    public static int firstMissingPositive(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;

        // Step 1: Place each number in its correct position
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (nums[i] > 0 && nums[i] <= n 
                   && nums[nums[i] - 1] != nums[i]) {

                int correctIndex = nums[i] - 1;

                // Swap nums[i] with nums[correctIndex]
                int temp = nums[i];
                nums[i] = nums[correctIndex];
                nums[correctIndex] = temp;
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Find the missing number
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i + 1)
                return i + 1;
        }

        return n + 1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3, 4, -1, 1};
        System.out.println(firstMissingPositive(arr)); // Output: 2
    }
}

